<section class="home-section" id="study-tips">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2 class="section-title">Smarter Homework, Less Stress</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">Habits that help whether you are in Year 3 or Year 11</p>
  </div>
  <div class="home-prose">
    <p>Most homework stress comes from not knowing where to start, not from the work itself. A few small habits make a big difference, and they cost nothing.</p>
    <ul class="home-list">
      <li><strong>Read the question twice.</strong> Underline the numbers and the word that tells you what to find, such as total, difference, area or average.</li>
      <li><strong>Estimate before you calculate.</strong> If 19 x 21 is about 20 x 20, you expect roughly 400. An answer of 4,000 should make you stop and look again.</li>
      <li><strong>Show every step.</strong> Teachers give marks for method, and written steps make mistakes easy to spot later.</li>
      <li><strong>Check with a second method.</strong> Solve it on paper, then confirm it with the calculator. When the two disagree, you have found the line worth a second look.</li>
      <li><strong>Read the topic, then take the quiz.</strong> Our explainers cover Maths, Science, History, English and more in plain language. The short quiz at the end shows you what has really stuck.</li>
      <li><strong>Little and often beats cramming.</strong> Twenty minutes a day on a tricky topic does more than a two-hour panic the night before a test.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Not sure where to begin? Start with <a href="/math">Maths</a> or <a href="/science">Science</a>, or type a topic into the search box at the top of the page.</p>
  </div>
</section>
